    "notice": "Answers whether an address is an author Safe holding `recordsModule`         as its records module.         Two allowlists are fixed at construction and there is no admin:         `singletons` (Safe singleton implementations) and `proxyCodehashes`         (the runtime code hash of Safe's proxy). Both must be verified         against the Safe deployments on the chain this gate is deployed to,         not guessed — a gate with a wrong code hash rejects every real Safe,         and a gate with a wrong singleton accepts a proxy pointed at a fake         implementation.         The order matters. The code hash is the anchor: any contract can         answer `masterCopy()`, but only Safe's proxy has the proxy runtime         code. The proxy then answers `masterCopy()` from its own dispatcher,         so a fake singleton contract cannot lie about which singleton a         contract that passed the code-hash test is proxying onto. Only then         is the module read: it must be enabled on the Safe, and the module         must answer that *this* Safe is its own.         No try/catch. A contract that passed the code-hash test is Safe's         proxy and its `masterCopy()` cannot revert; if the module's `safe()`         reverts, the call reverts and the caller is not an authorised author.         A module with no code is rejected before the call, so an EOA passed         as `recordsModule` is `false`, not a revert from an empty call.",
